---
import DocPage from '../../components/DocPage.astro'; import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'; import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro'; import CodeNotes from '../../components/CodeNotes.astro';
const gd = `extends Control

const GuaAutoAdapterScript := preload(
  "res://addons/gua/gua_auto_adapter.gd"
)

var ui := GuaAutoAdapterScript.new()

func _ready() -> void:
  ui.attach(self)
  ui.update("title")
  ui.start_inspector_bridge(8765)

func _process(_delta: float) -> void:
  ui.update("title")`;
---
<DocPage eyebrow="GODOT / UI" title="Install and implement UI" lead="Install the engine add-on, then connect UI built from standard Controls to the automatic collector." description="Install the Gua Godot addon and enable automatic UI collection." section="godot">
  <Callout kind="tip" title="Godot add-on and test packages"><p>Download <code>gua-godot-addon-v&lt;version&gt;.zip</code> from the <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/releases/latest">latest Gua release assets</a>. The ZIP contains native libraries for Windows x64, Linux x64, macOS x64/arm64, and Debug/Release WASM for Godot Web. Copy <code>addons/gua</code> into the game and add matching-version NuGet packages to the external test project. NuGet alone does not install the game add-on.</p><p><code>&lt;version&gt;</code> means the actual version shown in Assets; for 1.1.1, the file name contains 1.1.1. For an existing test setup, choose the release matching that setup.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Install the packaged add-on</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open the latest Gua release</strong><p>Download the Godot ZIP from the release assets and extract it.</p></li><li><strong>Copy addons/gua</strong><CodeBlock title="Expected layout" lang="text" code={`your-game/\n├─ project.godot\n└─ addons/\n   └─ gua/\n      ├─ gua.gdextension\n      ├─ gua_auto_adapter.gd\n      ├─ plugin.cfg\n      └─ bin/`} /><CodeNotes title="What each file does" items={[
    { code: 'project.godot', explanation: 'The main Godot project configuration.' },
    { code: 'addons/gua/gua.gdextension', explanation: 'Tells Godot how to load the native Gua extension.' },
    { code: 'addons/gua/gua_auto_adapter.gd', explanation: 'Collects Controls and routes external actions back into Godot.' },
    { code: 'addons/gua/plugin.cfg', explanation: 'Declares the add-on and its editor entry point.' },
    { code: 'addons/gua/bin/', explanation: 'Contains the platform-specific native libraries loaded by the extension.' },
  ]} /></li><li><strong>Open the project in Godot</strong><p>Check the Godot output to confirm that the GDExtension loaded successfully.</p></li></ol>
  <h2>Attach it from GDScript</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Preload the adapter explicitly</strong><p>Explicit preload avoids depending on <code>class_name</code> registration order.</p><CodeBlock title="main.gd" lang="gdscript" code={gd} /><CodeNotes title="Read main.gd from top to bottom" items={[
    { code: 'extends Control', explanation: 'Makes this script a root UI Control and a valid value for attach(self).' },
    { code: 'preload("res://addons/gua/gua_auto_adapter.gd")', explanation: 'Loads the adapter script explicitly before the scene starts.' },
    { code: 'var ui := GuaAutoAdapterScript.new()', explanation: 'Creates one adapter instance for collection and action dispatch.' },
    { code: 'func _ready() -> void:', explanation: 'Runs once after this Control and its children enter the scene tree.' },
    { code: 'ui.attach(self)', explanation: 'Sets this Control as the root; Controls below it become the collection scope.' },
    { code: 'ui.update("title")', explanation: 'Publishes the current Controls under the logical screen name title.' },
    { code: 'ui.start_inspector_bridge(8765)', explanation: 'Starts the WebSocket bridge used by Inspector, MCP, and external tests.' },
    { code: 'func _process(_delta: float) -> void:', explanation: 'Runs every rendered frame; the underscore marks the unused elapsed-time argument.' },
    { code: 'ui.update("title")', explanation: 'Re-collects state and dispatches queued external actions every frame.' },
  ]} /></li><li><strong>Pin IDs only where stability matters</strong><p>The default ID comes from the NodePath. Use metadata when an ID must survive scene-tree refactoring.</p><CodeBlock title="Stable and sensitive fields" lang="gdscript" code={`name_input.set_meta("gua_id", "login-user")\npassword_input.set_meta("gua_id", "login-password")\npassword_input.set_meta("gua_sensitive", true)`} /><CodeNotes title="What the metadata means" items={[
    { code: 'name_input.set_meta("gua_id", "login-user")', explanation: 'Pins a test-facing ID that survives NodePath refactoring.' },
    { code: 'password_input.set_meta("gua_id", "login-password")', explanation: 'Pins a stable ID for the password field.' },
    { code: 'password_input.set_meta("gua_sensitive", true)', explanation: 'Prevents plaintext from entering the semantic tree, retained history, or diagnostics. Mask rendered screenshots separately.' },
  ]} /></li></ol>
  <Callout title="No duplicate registration"><p>The adapter recursively collects standard Controls from the root. It observes button signals and routes external click requests back through normal Godot signals.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Publish a World Object Tree</h2><p>For a door, enemy, objective, or other non-UI object, add its <code>Node2D</code> or <code>Node3D</code> to the <code>gua_world_object</code> group and attach semantic metadata. The adapter collects position and state on each <code>ui.update()</code>, so game code does not call the native registration API directly.</p><CodeBlock title="Publish a door as a game-world object" lang="gdscript" code={`@onready var reactor_door: Node2D = $World/ReactorDoor
var reactor_door_locked := true

func expose_reactor_door() -> void:
  reactor_door.add_to_group(&"gua_world_object")
  reactor_door.set_meta(&"gua_world_id", "reactor-door")
  reactor_door.set_meta(&"gua_world_kind", "door")
  reactor_door.set_meta(&"gua_world_label", "Reactor door")
  reactor_door.set_meta(&"gua_world_visible_to_player", true)
  reactor_door.set_meta(&"gua_world_tags", ["reactor", "mission-critical"])
  update_reactor_door_state()

func update_reactor_door_state() -> void:
  reactor_door.set_meta(&"gua_world_state", {
    "locked": reactor_door_locked,
    "power": "online"
  })`} /><p>Call <code>expose_reactor_door()</code> once from <code>_ready()</code>, then call <code>update_reactor_door_state()</code> whenever the door changes. <code>gua_world_id</code> is required and unique within the frame. Position comes from <code>global_position</code>, and the nearest published ancestor becomes the parent. Keep state flat and limited to strings, finite numbers, booleans, or null. See <a href="/world-object-tree/">World Object Tree</a> for the conceptual model and query tools.</p>
  <h2>Export to Web</h2><p>The same released add-on supports Godot Web Debug and Release exports. In the Web export preset, enable <strong>Extension Support</strong> and use the single-threaded variant. Godot reads <code>gua.gdextension</code> and selects the matching <code>web.wasm32.single.debug</code> or <code>web.wasm32.single.release</code> WASM automatically.</p><Callout kind="tip" title="Browser-native AI tools"><p>A Web export installs <code>window.__guaGodotWebPort</code> in its page. Use the <a href="/webmcp/">browser-native WebMCP guide</a> to register Gua tools in the same tab. Serve the export over HTTP(S); do not open the generated HTML directly from disk.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Commonly observed Controls</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Inputs</strong><p>LineEdit, TextEdit, CheckBox, OptionButton, and ItemList</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>State</strong><p>Focus, selection, value, scrolling, ranges, visibility, and enabled state</p></div></div>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Port conflicts"><p>The bridge cannot start if another process already owns port <code>8765</code>. Look for <code>Failed to start Gua Inspector bridge</code> in the Godot output.</p></Callout>
  <h2 id="observe">Expose additional UI/World observations</h2><p>Register Observe values explicitly for typed data outside the standard tree. Attach them to a published UI/World owner and unregister when its object is removed. Use <code>GuaAutoAdapter.create_observe_owner</code> in game code. Player exposure is explicit opt-in. See <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/protocol/specs/observe-transport-v1.md">engine registration examples</a> and <a href="/docs/gua-runtime/#observe">Value, registration, and external subscriptions</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
